manipulating data in an Excel format.

54
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:58,380
So the basic difference between the close
and the adjusted close columns of data is
that the close column adjusts prices for
stock splits, whereas the adjusted close
column prices adjusts for splits and
dividends.

55
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:05,640
So let me briefly explain what both of
those mean in simple terms, starting with
stock split adjustments.

56
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:18,180
So if you, for example, have a stock with
a price that is $100 per share, let's say,
and the company then goes through a stock
split of two to one, meaning the share
base is doubled.

57
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:24,820
Then all else being equal, the price of
that share of each share will decrease to
$50.

58
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:28,900
However, nothing's actually happened to
the value of the company as a whole.

59
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:40,740
And in general, nothing happens to the
value of the stock each shareholder is
holding, since even though the value per
share has halved, they'll now hold double
the amount of shares.

60
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:49,860
If prices are adjusted for stock splits,
then the historical time series will show
prices based on whatever the current share
base is.

61
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:59,120
So in the example that I just described,
that would mean that the price series
would now show the price being $50 per
share, both before and after the stock
split.

62
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:11,740
And that basically makes sense because
there hasn't been a material change in the
value of the company itself, nor has the
event affected the value of individual
shareholders holdings.

63
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:24,000
A similar historical adjustment is made
for dividends in the adjusted close column
on top of the stock split adjustments that
we see in the closing prices.

64
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:36,860
So if a stock closes at $50, let's say,
and the next day begins trading X dividend
based on a $2 dividend, it's going to open
at roughly $48, all else being equal.

65
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:48,740
Now, this gap doesn't actually represent
pure market forces or the returns to the
individual shareholders, since the
shareholders will receive that $2 gap in
price in the form of a dividend.

66
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:58,480
So in general, for most of the analysis
that we do in this course, it doesn't
actually matter too much which one you
use, the closing price or the adjusted
closing price.

67
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:16,020
Since dividend adjustments typically have
a very small effect on overall returns,
when we're concerned with trading and
shorter term horizons, they are a bit more
of a concern, obviously, if you're looking
at investing or longer term horizons,
especially if you're looking to reinvest
those dividends.

68
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:27,980
However, you can argue that for the
purpose of analysis that involves total
returns or understanding pure market
forces on a stock, looking at adjusted
closing prices is slightly more accurate.

69
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:39,960
So the main takeaway here, really, the
point that I want to get across is that so
long as you understand the data, what
you're looking at, and you're happy with
using it in the context of whatever
analysis you're carrying out, then that's
fine.

70
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:44,720
You need to get used to being flexible
with sourcing and using data.

