156
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:26,920
So let's go through that plotting and
moving average is very simple.

157
00:20:26,980 --> 00:20:30,140
Basically, Excel already has this
functionality built in.

158
00:20:30,680 --> 00:20:45,200
All you need to do is select the chart
line and then click on design in the
ribbon, followed by add chart element, and
then choose moving average from the trend
line options that you see in that drop
down.

159
00:20:45,840 --> 00:20:51,940
With that done, you can then go to format
on the ribbon and select the trend line
element.

160
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,280
that you just created in the top left drop
down and click format selection.

161
00:20:56,280 --> 00:21:02,340
And then what you're going to see is
basically a format trend line panel appear
on the right hand side here at the
worksheet.

162
00:21:02,340 --> 00:21:06,280
And from there, you can change the line
color and style.

163
00:21:06,280 --> 00:21:14,260
And importantly, if you select the trend
line options tab, you can change the
number of periods the moving average is
determined by.

164
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:20,540
So for this example, we're looking at
daily data.

165
00:21:20,540 --> 00:21:32,540
If we want to create a three month moving
average, we'd enter 60 into the period box
because using daily data here, there are
20 periods or 20 trading days in each
month.

166
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:37,140
So a three month moving average would be
60 periods.

167
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:44,820
If you want to draw on trend lines or
things like support and resistance, you
can also do that quite easily.

168
00:21:44,820 --> 00:21:50,100
First, you need to make sure that you've
selected the plot area by left clicking
it.

169
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:58,100
And then to draw shapes within that plot
area, you simply go to the insert tab on
the ribbon and choose whichever shape you
want.

170
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:07,380
So for example, a line, you can then draw
that line by left clicking and dragging
your cursor to create starting and ending
points of the line.

171
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:14,860
And by doing it in this way, what we're
making sure of is that the lines and the
shapes that you draw will move around with
the chart area.

172
00:22:14,860 --> 00:22:18,080
So that's why we selected the plot area
first.

173
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:26,720
However, when you do update the chart with
more data, you will probably need to make
some edits to the lines that you draw to
maintain their accuracy,

174
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:31,060
and which you can do easily by clicking
and dragging the nodes at each end of the
line.

175
00:22:31,060 --> 00:22:41,500
Once it's selected, sometimes it's useful
as well to have various timeframes charted
for a particular asset or economic
indicator,

176
00:22:41,500 --> 00:22:47,100
whatever data you're looking at, rather
than looking at the entire historical data
set.

177
00:22:47,540 --> 00:22:55,420
And an easy way that I kind of do this is
just to copy a chart that you've already
made like this one here and then edit the
date axis

178
00:22:55,420 --> 00:22:57,460
and the value axis is desired.

179
00:22:57,460 --> 00:23:03,920
So for example, if we want to create a
chart that just looks at the data from
2019 onwards,

180
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:12,340
we can just select the chart that we
already have and then copy and paste it
with either the right click functions I
showed earlier,

181
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:15,640
or as I tend to use just the keyboard
shortcuts.

182
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:28,020
So copy with control C, followed by
control V to paste it once that new
charts, then here as it is now, you can
sort of size it, position it how you want.

183
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:36,560
And you can double click the date axis to
format it and change the minimum date as
we're going to do in this example to 2019.

184
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:45,060
Obviously, you can put that to any date
you wish, so long as you've got the data
for that date, it's probably also a good
idea in this example,

185
00:23:45,060 --> 00:23:58,980
to change or reset the intervals, because
the previous intervals that we use were
for a lot longer data set and they're not
really sort of relevant or they don't
really work that well for a much smaller
data set.

186
00:23:58,980 --> 00:24:07,480
So from prices just from 2019 onwards, so
we can make them more relevant just by it,
by changing that quickly now.

187
00:24:08,860 --> 00:24:10,860
So I think one month will do.

188
00:24:12,060 --> 00:24:16,560
And you can also change the scaling for
the value axis at the moment.

189
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:25,480
You can see the price values go all the
way up to $120 a share because that's the
automatic maximum based on the entire data
data series we have available.

190
00:24:25,480 --> 00:24:33,280
However, you can manually override that if
