looking at a little bit clearer.

191
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:46,100
And to do that, you just double click on
the value axis and you can choose the
values maximum and minimum values that you
can see here just by overwriting the
values that you see in the boxes.

192
00:24:46,100 --> 00:24:53,080
So for the maximum, we might change it
from 120 in this example to 30.

193
00:24:53,080 --> 00:25:03,420
You can also, as I said, change the
minimum here, which might be suitable for
stocks with a much higher share price,
just to sort of get the scaling right.

194
00:25:03,420 --> 00:25:06,520
So you can see the changes in price more
easily over time.

195
00:25:07,520 --> 00:25:21,340
You'll notice that the trend line that's
being copied across from the first chart
to the second chart has not adjusted to
the changes that we've made when we've
changed the sort of window of data that
we're looking at.

196
00:25:21,340 --> 00:25:23,680
And, you know, scaled both axes.

197
00:25:23,680 --> 00:25:34,680
So that needs to be edited, as I said
earlier, and you can do that really easily
just by selecting the line and clicking
and dragging the nodes at each end to the
desired locations.

198
00:25:34,680 --> 00:25:41,020
Okay, so hopefully, all of that formatting
stuff makes sense and was easy enough to
follow.

199
00:25:41,020 --> 00:25:55,020
The final thing that I want to know about
these stock sheets is that in order to
update them, all you need to do is copy
and paste some new price data over the old
data, as long as it's from the same source
and in the same format.

200
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:58,360
So, you know, in this case, from Yahoo
Finance.

201
00:25:58,360 --> 00:26:01,360
So let's take an example.

202
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:17,360
If we consider a scenario where we hadn't
updated this price data since the end of
2020 and so we want to update it with all
of the new 2021 data, then we would start
by, I'll sort of simulate this.

203
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:22,700
So I'll start by deleting all of the post
2021 data.

204
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:28,700
So we're imagining now we've only got data
up to the end of 2020.

205
00:26:28,700 --> 00:26:42,020
And assuming we're in this position and we
want to update this stock for all of the
2021 data, all you'd need to do is
download the data from the same source in
the same format and copy those columns of
data into this sheet.

206
00:26:42,020 --> 00:26:48,360
So columns A to G in this case, that would
mean downloading and updating the data
from Yahoo Finance.

207
00:26:48,360 --> 00:26:52,360
As I said, copying columns A to G over
just as we did earlier.

208
00:26:52,360 --> 00:26:55,360
And so I'll just do that quickly.

209
00:26:55,360 --> 00:27:08,360
Now, if we go back to the FireEye data
sheet that we downloaded from Yahoo
Finance earlier and carry out that process
here, I'm just copying columns A to G and
pasting them into the FireEye sheet in
this portfolio modeling sheet.

210
00:27:08,360 --> 00:27:16,700
And you can see now that all the charts
and everything is updated to reflect that
new data.

211
00:27:16,700 --> 00:27:18,700
And that's all there is to it, really.

212
00:27:18,700 --> 00:27:39,160
That's also, you know, the main reason why
we made sure that when we entered the cell
ranges for our data series in the charts
and our date series as well, we made sure
that they would encompass data a lot
further down the sheet than our initial
data set.

213
00:27:39,160 --> 00:27:47,500
So remember, we chose down to row 100,000
that so that it can make sure it updates
with new data like we've just kind of
proven here.

214
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:50,500
So that's it for the first section of the
video.

215
00:27:50,500 --> 00:27:54,840
And you now know how to download and plot
stock price data in Excel.

216
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:57,840
All fairly straightforward so far, but it
is useful stuff.

217
00:27:57,840 --> 00:28:04,840
And everything we go through is just going
to act as building blocks as you learn
more and more through the course.

218
00:28:04,840 --> 00:28:13,360
Let's move on to the next section now,
which is where we're going to look at how
to compare the performance of two stocks
in the form of a spread trade.

219
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:28,700
And what I mean by that is a pairs trade
where we're looking at going long one
stock versus short another, the same
principles that we're going to cover here
can be applied across other assets as
well, if that's something that you wish to
do.

220
00:28:28,700 --> 00:28:38,360
So when we look at one stock, it's fairly
obvious how to assess its performance and
all you need to do really is look at its
price or total returns over time.

221
00:28:38,360 --> 00:28:48,360
However, if you're looking at two stocks
in the form of a long and a short
position, we need to find a way of
combining their individual performance
into a single data series.

222
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:57,320
And what we're going to look at in this
section are various different methods of
doing that using fresh pair and dollar
tree stocks as examples.

223
00:28:57,320 --> 00:29:01,520
The first couple of methods I want to show
you are quite crude, but easy to carry
out.

224
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:12,960
And then I'm going to go through what are
essentially a couple more accurate
approaches which can be carried forward to
analyzing performance at the portfolio
level with more than two stocks.

225
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:15,520
And that's something that we're going to
be doing later in the video.

226
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:21,280
So the first two calculations I'm going to
use here are what's called the spread and
ratio methods.

227
00:29:21,280 --> 00:29:26,160
And as always, the first step here is to
obtain the data that we need.

228
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:34,480
In this case, we need the price series
data for fresh pair and dollar tree, and
we need to create a common time series for
both of them.

229
00:29:34,880 --> 00:29:44,240
That basically means that we need to make
sure that we only use price data when it's
available to both stocks on any given day.

230
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:54,960
So let's start by copying and pasting the
price and date series for each stock into
this blank sheet here, spread and ratio
analysis from the relevant individual
stock tabs.

231
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:59,280
So starting with fresh pair, the ticker is
FRPT.

232
00:29:59,280 --> 00:30:01,280
So we can navigate to that sheet.

233
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:09,680
And all we need to do here is select both
the date and the adjusted close columns by
holding down control on the keyboard and
left clicking on those columns.

234
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:20,400
And with them both selected, I'm just
going to copy them with control C and then
paste them into control V into columns A
and B of the spread and ratio analysis
sheet.

235
00:30:21,280 --> 00:30:26,720
Similarly, for dollar tree now, that's
DLTR, the ticker.

236
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:31,120
So we can go to the DLTR sheet and we can
do the same thing.

237
00:30:31,120 --> 00:30:37,760
And I'll copy those columns into columns E
and F of the spread and ratio analysis
sheet.

238
00:30:38,720 --> 00:30:44,960
Before we go any further, let's rename the
columns B and F to reflect the tickers for
those two stocks.

239
00:30:44,960 --> 00:30:55,120
So in cell B2, I'm just going to type FRPT
for fresh pet and in cell F2, I'm going to
type DLTR for dollar tree.

240
00:30:56,480 --> 00:31:05,360
Now, if we look at the time series here,
we can see quite clearly that the data
series that we have for dollar tree goes
back to 1995,

241
00:31:05,360 --> 00:31:10,720
whilst the series for fresh pet is a lot
shorter since the dates only go back to
2014.

242
00:31:11,520 --> 00:31:18,160
To compare these two stocks fairly, we
need to only use price data on dates that
are shared by both assets,

243
00:31:18,160 --> 00:31:23,840
which basically means we can only use
price data corresponding to the shorter
time series.

244
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:30,960
To make sure that we're matching price
data from the dollar tree series with the
dates that we see in the fresh pet series,

245
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:35,200
we can use an Excel lookup function called
VLOOKUP.

246
00:31:35,200 --> 00:31:39,200
So in cell C2 here, I'm going to type
equals VLOOKUP.

247
00:31:39,200 --> 00:31:40,160
That's our function.

248
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:46,720
Then open brackets because we always need
to fill out the parameters for our
function and you put those in brackets.

249
00:31:47,360 --> 00:31:50,480
And we've got four parameters to fill in
here.

250
00:31:50,480 --> 00:31:53,600
The first is choosing the date that we
want to look up.

251
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:55,280
That's going to be cell A2.

252
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:00,960
Then I'm going to put in a comma and
choose columns E to F,

253
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:04,240
which is the data table that we're going
to look within.

254
00:32:04,240 --> 00:32:07,440
And then I'm going to put another comma
and put in two,

255
00:32:07,440 --> 00:32:11,280
because we're going to look in the second
column of that data table.

256
00:32:11,280 --> 00:32:18,560
And then finally another comma followed by
FALSE, which is just a parameter that
makes sure we look up the correct date.

257
00:32:19,120 --> 00:32:23,360
So let me explain what this does a little
bit more clearly.

258
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:28,400
So the function performs a vertical lookup
of the value that we see in cell A2,

259
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:30,800
which is our date from the fresh pet
series.

260
00:32:30,800 --> 00:32:36,800
And it looks it up in the table array of
columns E to F, which is our dollar tree
data.

261
00:32:37,680 --> 00:32:42,880
It then returns the value in the second
column of that array that we specified.

262
00:32:42,880 --> 00:32:48,160
And the FALSE parameter at the end of the
function just makes sure that it only
returns an exact match

263
00:32:48,160 --> 00:32:50,080
for the date that we're looking up.

264
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:56,400
So again, just to be clear, the function
is looking up a date from the fresh pet
series column B.

265
00:32:56,400 --> 00:32:59,680
It's finding it in the dollar tree series
column E.

266
00:32:59,680 --> 00:33:04,000
It then returns the corresponding price
from the dollar tree series in column F.

267
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:11,360
And this function basically just makes
sure that we're lining up prices of the
two stocks correctly.

268
00:33:12,320 --> 00:33:13,760
So with that function inputted,

269
00:33:13,760 --> 00:33:18,320
we can just copy it down to find all the
dollar tree prices on those particular
dates.

270
00:33:19,600 --> 00:33:22,800
So now we know that our prices are aligned
properly.

271
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:24,560
Let's tidy things up a little bit.

272
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:29,120
We want to be able to delete the dollar
tree data series in columns E to F.

273
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:32,560
Now, since we've aligned those prices
properly in column C.

274
00:33:32,560 --> 00:33:38,160
However, if we delete columns E to F, then
the formulas that we've created in column
C

275
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:43,600
will return errors because they can no
longer look up prices from the deleted
columns.

276
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:47,920
So to get around this problem, all we need
to do is convert the formulas in column C

277
00:33:48,640 --> 00:33:50,800
to their hard outputs or values.

278
00:33:50,800 --> 00:33:56,880
And the easiest way to do this is by
copying the column and pasting the values
over themselves,

279
00:33:56,880 --> 00:34:00,080
but as values instead of the formulas.

280
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:04,800
To do that, just copy column C and then
right click within that selection

281
00:34:04,800 --> 00:34:09,280
and select the paste as values clipboard
option from the dropdown menu.

282
00:34:09,280 --> 00:34:15,840
And you can see now if I click on one of
the cells that previously had our lookup
formulas in

283
00:34:15,840 --> 00:34:21,360
that we specified earlier, it now just has
the output values of those formulas.

284
00:34:21,360 --> 00:34:26,400
So with that done, we can safely delete
columns E to F now by selecting them,

285
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:29,040
right clicking within the selection and
choosing delete.

286
00:34:29,040 --> 00:34:34,640
Don't forget as well to give column C a
title, which is for this example,

287
00:34:34,640 --> 00:34:37,680
it can just be DLTR, the ticker for dollar
tree.

288
00:34:40,480 --> 00:34:43,360
Now we've got our price data ready for
both stocks.

289
00:34:43,360 --> 00:34:47,840
Let's talk about what spread and ratio
calculation methods actually are.

290
00:34:48,720 --> 00:34:53,040
A spread is just the price difference
between two assets, meaning for example,

291
00:34:53,040 --> 00:34:56,080
subtracting the price of dollar tree from
fresh pet.

292
00:34:56,080 --> 00:34:59,840
And a ratio is the price of one asset
divided by another.

293
00:34:59,840 --> 00:35:05,200
So for example, the price of fresh pet
divided by the price of dollar tree.

294
00:35:05,200 --> 00:35:08,960
Both of these methods are simple ways to
create a common time series that you can

295
00:35:08,960 --> 00:35:12,400
use to track the performance of trading a
long short pair.

296
00:35:13,280 --> 00:35:19,280
As a side note though, when you're
comparing a long idea versus a short idea
by using a spread or

297
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:24,720
ratio index, the convention is always to
have your long idea as the first variable
in the equation,

298
00:35:24,720 --> 00:35:29,280
meaning for a spread calculation, you take
the long price minus the short price.

299
00:35:29,280 --> 00:35:33,440
And for a ratio calculation, you take the
long price divided by the short price.

300
00:35:34,240 --> 00:35:38,080
So let's calculate the spread and ratio
time series for these stocks now,

301
00:35:38,080 --> 00:35:44,080
based on the assumption that our long idea
is fresh pet and our short idea is dollar
tree.

302
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:49,120
In column D, I'm going to calculate the
spread and in cell D1,

303
00:35:49,120 --> 00:35:53,760
I'm just going to head the column with
FRPT minus DLTR.

304
00:35:54,480 --> 00:35:59,760
Below that heading in cell D2, we need to
write in a formula as follows.

305
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:04,640
And that's just going to be equals and
then the cell reference B2 minus C2.

306
00:36:04,640 --> 00:36:11,520
So you can just type in equals and then
select B2, then hit minus on the keyboard
and select cell C2.

307
00:36:12,960 --> 00:36:18,160
That's just the price of fresh pet on the
date in row two.

308
00:36:18,160 --> 00:36:24,560
So the 7th of November, 2014 minus the
price of dollar tree on that same date.

309
00:36:25,120 --> 00:36:32,160
And when we copy this formula down by
double clicking the little green box in
the bottom right of the cell selection,

310
00:36:32,160 --> 00:36:36,960
that'll copy down that formula for the
relevant dates all the way down to the end
of the data series.

311
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:45,760
Once you do that, always make sure when
you copy a formula down or across a sheet
that you check the data afterwards.

312
00:36:45,760 --> 00:36:50,880
And the easiest way to do that is to
navigate to the end of the series where
you've copied down or across.

313
00:36:50,880 --> 00:36:56,480
And here I'm just doing that by pressing
control and down on the keyboard to
quickly get to the end of the data series.

314
00:36:57,120 --> 00:37:06,320
And then double click on the final
calculation cell and just make sure that
you're happy with the references and how
the formula is working.

315
00:37:07,600 --> 00:37:11,120
Now let's move on to ratio calculations
then.

316
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:19,600
In cell E1, I'm going to title the column
FRPT forward slash, so divided by DLTR.

317
00:37:19,600 --> 00:37:26,560
And in the cell below, the formula this
time is going to be equals B2 divided by
C2.

318
00:37:27,120 --> 00:37:35,520
And as before, we need to copy down that
formula to the end of the series and check
it as well as we did for column D.

319
00:37:35,520 --> 00:37:39,520
And you can see here that all the
calculations look correct.

320
00:37:40,320 --> 00:37:46,640
They've yielded values to multiple decimal
places, so the formatting is a little
untidy.

321
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:57,840
What we can do is just format that column
of data by selecting column E and
right-clicking within the selection and
then going to format cells.

322
00:37:58,720 --> 00:38:04,160
Choosing the number option and then
formatting the numbers to three decimal
places.

323
00:38:07,680 --> 00:38:08,560
Okay, great.

324
00:38:08,560 --> 00:38:17,120
We've now got the underlying data sorted,
but before I move on to explaining how and
when spread and ratio calculations are
useful,

325
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:22,000
we need to create some charts to
illustrate the new data series that we've
calculated.

326
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:26,640
Now we've already been through the
charting process, so I'll make this one
quite quick.

327
00:38:27,200 --> 00:38:31,040
I want to create a line chart to plot the
spread series first.

328
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:36,160
And so I'm just going to select insert on
the ribbon, followed by 2D line chart.

329
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:43,680
And from there, I'll move and resize the
chart and then right-click within the
chart area and go to select data.

330
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:52,480
Within that dialog box, I'm now going to
choose to add a new series and then select
the series name as cell D1

331
00:38:52,480 --> 00:38:57,520
and the series values as the entire series
we calculated in column D.

332
00:38:57,520 --> 00:39:02,000
So that would be all the data from cell D2
down to the end of the data series.

333
00:39:02,560 --> 00:39:09,200
Now, I know in the previous example, we
extended this to blank rows well beyond
the data series.

334
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:14,800
Obviously, you can do that if you think
you're going to continuously update this
chart with new data.

335
00:39:14,800 --> 00:39:20,800
In this case, I don't think that's
particularly relevant, so I'm just going
to leave it there at the end of the data
set.

336
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:31,520
So next, what you want to do is add our
horizontal axis labels, which we can do
again from inside the select data source
dialog box.

337
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:39,520
And to do that, you click on the edit
horizontal axis button and then choose the
corresponding dates for all the spread
series data.

338
00:39:39,520 --> 00:39:45,440
So in this case, that's going to be all
the dates from cell A2 down to the end of
the data series again.

339
00:39:45,440 --> 00:39:51,440
And that's all there is to selecting data
sources for our chart.

340
00:39:51,440 --> 00:39:55,760
So you can click OK to come out of those
options now and we can see the chart
there.

341
00:39:55,760 --> 00:40:01,600
And we're basically done now aside from
