356
00:41:20,560 --> 00:41:26,720
horizontal axis labels and select cell A2
to the end of the series to add our dates.

357
00:41:29,600 --> 00:41:34,640
Okay, now we've run the calculations and
created charts for our new series.

358
00:41:34,640 --> 00:41:38,880
Let's talk about how and when the spread
and ratio calculations can be useful.

359
00:41:39,680 --> 00:41:44,960
If we take a look at the spread first
between the two stocks, as I said
previously, this is just the

360
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:48,000
difference between the price per share of
the two stocks.

361
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:56,080
So essentially what the data shows is the
performance of going long one share of
fresh pet and short one share of dollar
tree.

362
00:41:56,560 --> 00:42:01,600
Now, obviously, this can be extrapolated
as well to two or three or any number of
shares,

363
00:42:01,600 --> 00:42:05,440
as long as they're equal on both the long
and short side.

364
00:42:05,440 --> 00:42:07,760
So let's take an example now.

365
00:42:07,760 --> 00:42:12,160
In July 2020, the spread between these
stocks was roughly zero,

366
00:42:12,160 --> 00:42:16,080
meaning that one share for each company
cost around the same price.

367
00:42:16,080 --> 00:42:24,240
But by February 2021, fresh pet cost
around $60 more than DLTR than dollar
tree,

368
00:42:24,240 --> 00:42:29,520
which tells us that if you were long one
share of fresh pet and short one share of
dollar tree,

369
00:42:29,520 --> 00:42:33,040
you would have made about $60 over the
period.

370
00:42:33,040 --> 00:42:38,480
Not including, of course, any fees that
you would pay along the way, trading costs
and things like that.

371
00:42:39,440 --> 00:42:45,760
So equally as well, if you were to go long
and short two shares of fresh pet and
dollar tree,

372
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:51,360
respectively, you would have made $120,
which is, of course, $60 times by two.

373
00:42:52,160 --> 00:42:54,960
So that's what spread performance does.

374
00:42:54,960 --> 00:43:00,480
It shows us the performance in dollar per
share terms of one stock versus another,

375
00:43:00,480 --> 00:43:04,320
provided that there's an equal number of
shares applied to both assets.

376
00:43:05,360 --> 00:43:08,080
The ratio calculation is a bit different.

377
00:43:08,080 --> 00:43:12,800
In this case, mathematically, what you're
calculating is the price of the long stock

378
00:43:12,800 --> 00:43:14,960
as a percentage of the price of the short
stock.

379
00:43:15,760 --> 00:43:23,200
And this method is regularly used in
finance as a measure of out or under
performance between one

380
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:28,400
asset and another based on both stocks
being equally weighted in dollar terms.

381
00:43:28,400 --> 00:43:33,440
So not in share terms in the actual dollar
capital committed to each side of the
trade.

382
00:43:34,320 --> 00:43:40,560
Unfortunately, it is also sometimes
mistakenly used as an exact measure of the
constant equal

383
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:45,840
weighted performance you would achieve if
you were to implement a long short pairs
trade like this.

384
00:43:46,560 --> 00:43:48,960
I'm going to talk a bit more about that
later.

385
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:55,040
But equally weighted just means that the
same amount of dollars are committed to
each side of the trade

386
00:43:55,040 --> 00:43:59,360
and constant in this case just means that
the weighting doesn't change over time.

387
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:07,040
And I say mistakenly used because whilst a
ratio calculation does offer a very good
indication

388
00:44:07,040 --> 00:44:12,800
of the directional performance of the
trade, you can't estimate your percentage
returns from it.

389
00:44:12,800 --> 00:44:17,440
And if you did, they would end up being
grossly exaggerated one way or the other.

390
00:44:18,320 --> 00:44:23,680
However, that being said, the ratio method
does have a very high correlation

391
00:44:23,680 --> 00:44:27,920
to more accurate measures of equal
weighting or equally weighted performance.

392
00:44:27,920 --> 00:44:32,880
And that is why, as I said before, it is
often used as a quick way to gauge

393
00:44:32,880 --> 00:44:35,840
whether one stock is outperforming another
or not.

394
00:44:35,840 --> 00:44:42,320
So, for example, the way you might use a
ratio is if we look at the ratio chart
here

395
00:44:42,320 --> 00:44:46,400
for fresh pet versus dollar tree, whenever
the line is going up,

396
00:44:46,400 --> 00:44:51,840
we know that our long, so fresh pet in
this case, is outperforming our short and
vice versa.

397
00:44:51,840 --> 00:44:58,080
And that's really mostly what a ratio is
actually good for.

398
00:44:58,800 --> 00:45:09,280
You cannot use the ratio as a way of
calculating the percentage change or the
percentage returns that

399
00:45:09,280 --> 00:45:10,320
you would achieve.

400
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:15,360
So basically, you can't use the ratio to
measure the percentage change of the ratio

401
00:45:15,360 --> 00:45:20,480
and equate that to the percentage returns
you would see if you put on those two
positions.

402
00:45:20,480 --> 00:45:26,400
If you want to be able to precisely assess
the historical performance of a long
position versus

403
00:45:26,400 --> 00:45:32,080
a short position, then probably the
fairest way to do so is to work out the
percentage returns for

404
00:45:32,080 --> 00:45:38,960
each position over every period and then
apply equal weightings to the long and
short sides before

405
00:45:38,960 --> 00:45:42,000
then, of course, combining them into an
overall return.

406
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:46,560
In our data set, we're looking at daily
intervals.

407
00:45:46,560 --> 00:45:51,760
So what that means is that for every
trading day, we want to calculate the
daily percentage

408
00:45:51,760 --> 00:45:53,520
return for each stock.

409
00:45:53,520 --> 00:45:59,520
And once we've done that, all we do is
create an overall return for each period
by summing the

410
00:45:59,520 --> 00:46:03,600
equally weighted returns of both the long
and short positions.

411
00:46:04,640 --> 00:46:09,440
This methodology that I'm talking about is
sometimes referred to as a constant
equally weighted

412
00:46:09,440 --> 00:46:14,720
approach because for every calculation
date, the returns for each position are
equally weighted,

413
00:46:14,720 --> 00:46:20,320
which means that on every day, we're
assuming that an equal amount of capital
in dollar terms is

414
00:46:20,320 --> 00:46:21,520
committed to each position.

415
00:46:22,080 --> 00:46:27,840
Now, practically speaking, this isn't very
reasonable since whenever stock prices
move,

416
00:46:27,840 --> 00:46:29,920
the weightings will become imbalanced.

417
00:46:29,920 --> 00:46:35,920
And so realistically, you would need to
rebalance the weightings every day to
implement this properly.

418
00:46:36,800 --> 00:46:42,320
However, it does mean that when looking at
a time series of performance,

419
00:46:42,320 --> 00:46:46,160
the two positions are always fairly
assessed in terms of their relative
performance.

420
00:46:46,960 --> 00:46:54,320
So let's go ahead and do this analysis for
our long FRPT and short DLTR trade so that
we can compare

421
00:46:54,320 --> 00:46:58,320
our results to the work that we've already
done with the spread and ratio analysis.

422
00:46:59,200 --> 00:47:05,680
So to start with, in the performance CEW
sheet, I'm going to copy over the price
data that we used

423
00:47:05,680 --> 00:47:11,280
in the spread and ratio analysis sheet
just by selecting the right columns and
copying them across.

424
00:47:12,480 --> 00:47:19,280
From there, I want to basically calculate
the daily returns for each of these stocks
across the entire

425
00:47:19,280 --> 00:47:20,000
time series.

426
00:47:20,560 --> 00:47:26,640
So starting with Fresh Pet, in column D,
I'm going to title the column FRPT
percent.

427
00:47:27,280 --> 00:47:36,720
And in cell D3, I'm going to type in a
formula which is equals cell B3 divided by
cell B2 minus 1.

428
00:47:38,240 --> 00:47:43,520
This formula basically calculates the
percentage change in price from cell B2 to
B3.

429
00:47:43,520 --> 00:47:48,960
So that's the closing price on the 7th of
November to the closing price on the 10th
of November.

430
00:47:49,440 --> 00:47:54,080
And you might have noticed there that
those dates aren't sequential, okay?

431
00:47:54,640 --> 00:47:58,720
Not sequential calendar dates anyway, but
they are sequential trading days because

432
00:47:59,360 --> 00:48:01,040
those dates are over a weekend.

433
00:48:01,840 --> 00:48:06,640
Now, one thing to note here is that our
data is arranged in ascending order.

434
00:48:06,640 --> 00:48:10,160
So what that means is that the oldest data
is at the top of the sheet

435
00:48:10,160 --> 00:48:12,560
and the most recent data is at the bottom
of the sheet.

436
00:48:13,120 --> 00:48:17,520
And so the first formula that we've put in
is in cell D3, not D2.

437
00:48:18,320 --> 00:48:24,640
And that's because to calculate the
percentage returns for each day or for
each period,

438
00:48:24,640 --> 00:48:29,920
we need the data from the prior period,
which wouldn't have existed if we started
in the top row.

439
00:48:30,560 --> 00:48:35,040
So with this formula in place, we can copy
it down as we've done many times before,

440
00:48:35,040 --> 00:48:40,960
and then double check that the formula
looks good at the end of the series, which
you can see it does.

441
00:48:42,160 --> 00:48:47,120
So let's now go through the same process
for dollar tree returns,

442
00:48:47,120 --> 00:48:50,560
except we don't need to retype the formula
in here.

443
00:48:50,560 --> 00:48:57,280
And instead, what we can do is just copy
across the formula that we've got in cell
D3 to cell E3.

444
00:48:57,280 --> 00:49:03,760
And to do this, you just click and select
cell D3 and then drag the little green
mark in the bottom

445
00:49:03,760 --> 00:49:07,360
right of that cell selection across to the
new cell E3.

446
00:49:08,000 --> 00:49:13,200
Just in the same way as we might have done
if we copied down, you can also copy
across in this way.

447
00:49:13,840 --> 00:49:20,560
So if we then double click in the formula
just to check it, you can see that the
cell references

448
00:49:20,560 --> 00:49:26,960
have moved appropriately by one column to
reference the dollar tree prices instead
of fresh pet prices,

449
00:49:26,960 --> 00:49:33,440
so we can feel confident with that
formula. And with that done, again, we can
copy the formula

450
00:49:33,440 --> 00:49:37,600
down to the end of the data series just by
double clicking the little green box in
the bottom right

451
00:49:37,600 --> 00:49:46,160
of the cell selection. And before I go any
further, let's just format these columns
of returns to

452
00:49:46,160 --> 00:49:50,800
display themselves as percentages, because
actually, at the moment, they're a bit
hard to read just as

453
00:49:50,800 --> 00:49:56,320
decimals. So we can do that just by
selecting the columns, right clicking
within the selection and

454
00:49:56,320 --> 00:50:02,960
choosing format cells. And then we select
the percentage option. And for this
example, we'll round the

455
00:50:02,960 --> 00:50:11,840
percentages to two decimal places. Now, as
I said earlier, we need to combine these
two sets of

456
00:50:11,840 --> 00:50:16,640
returns into an overall return that
measures the performance of our long
versus our short, which

457
00:50:16,640 --> 00:50:22,880
I'm going to do in column F. To begin
with, I'm going to title the column CEW
percent, meaning

458
00:50:22,880 --> 00:50:30,320
constant equal weighting percentage. The
overall percentage returns for this
portfolio of two stocks is

459
00:50:30,320 --> 00:50:36,080
easy to calculate. All we need to do is
apply an equal weight to the long and
short returns and add them

460
00:50:36,080 --> 00:50:42,080
together. So there's two things we need to
do here. First, at the moment, we've
calculated the returns for

461
00:50:42,080 --> 00:50:48,080
both the stocks we're looking at as if
they're long positions. And remember,
we're assuming we're long,

462
00:50:48,080 --> 00:50:53,840
fresh pet, but short dollar tree in this
example. And so when we combine the
returns, we need to make

463
00:50:53,840 --> 00:51:00,560
sure that we invert the dollar tree
returns and make them negative so that
they equate to being short

464
00:51:00,560 --> 00:51:06,960
dollar tree. Second, if we're going to
equal weight the two positions, we need to
make sure that we apply

465
00:51:06,960 --> 00:51:13,600
a 50% weighting to each set of returns,
which would then together equate to 100%
for the spread trade

466
00:51:13,600 --> 00:51:20,080
as a whole. As I said before, this sort of
constant equal weighting approach is the
fairest way to

467
00:51:20,080 --> 00:51:25,840
calculate relative performance between the
two assets, but it isn't always the most
realistic option.

468
00:51:25,840 --> 00:51:34,640
And I'm going to touch on that a bit more
later. So anyway, in cell F3, the formula
for our combined

469
00:51:34,640 --> 00:51:48,800
returns is just going to be 0.5 times by
cell D3, close brackets, and then adding
in brackets 0.5 times

470
00:51:49,360 --> 00:51:57,040
minus cell E3, close brackets. And if we
look at this formula more closely, all
we're doing here is

471
00:51:57,040 --> 00:52:03,520
basically adding together two components,
a 50% weighting to the long fresh pet
returns and a 50%

472
00:52:03,520 --> 00:52:09,440
weighting to the short dollar tree
returns. And the reason why we have a
negative sign before

473
00:52:09,440 --> 00:52:16,320
referencing the dollar tree returns is to
transform those returns to represent a
short position in the

474
00:52:16,320 --> 00:52:23,280
stock. So that effectively gives us our
returns for the combination of the two
positions. And as you

475
00:52:23,280 --> 00:52:29,040
guys are going to see shortly, this is
pretty much identical to the approach that
we're going to take

476
00:52:29,040 --> 00:52:34,560
to calculating constant equal weighted
portfolio returns later in the video. The
difference being

477
00:52:34,560 --> 00:52:40,240
here is that this is just an example of a
portfolio of just two positions instead of
many, as we're

478
00:52:40,240 --> 00:52:47,280
going to see later. Okay, let's move on.
If you want to chart the performance of
this long short pair,

479
00:52:47,280 --> 00:52:52,160
we need to create an index, which is
another useful skill actually when it
comes to doing your own

480
00:52:52,160 --> 00:52:59,760
trading analyses in general. So in cell
G1, I'm going to title the column CEW
index. And below that

481
00:52:59,760 --> 00:53:07,040
in cell G2, we can start the index at any
value we wish. And common practice is to
start indices from

482
00:53:07,040 --> 00:53:12,880
a value of 100. So that's the value that
I'm going to enter into cell G2 here. And
that's going to

483
00:53:12,880 --> 00:53:22,160
represent the index value of this mini
portfolio on the 7th of November. Now in
cell G3, to calculate

484
00:53:22,160 --> 00:53:29,600
the index value on the 10th of November,
all we need to do is multiply the previous
index value, so 100,

485
00:53:29,600 --> 00:53:36,080
by 1 plus the returns for that period. So
what that's going to mean is in cell G3,
I'm going to type in the

486
00:53:36,080 --> 00:53:46,960
formula, equals cell G2, multiplied by, in
brackets, 1 plus cell F3. And so that's
just going to be the

487
00:53:46,960 --> 00:53:55,840
previous index value G2, multiplied by 1
plus the portfolio returns, or the
combined returns for the

488
00:53:55,840 --> 00:54:03,120
two positions, given that they're equally
weighted. So as we've done previously, we
can copy this formula

489
00:54:03,120 --> 00:54:09,920
down as well and format it appropriately.
I'm going to format the column this time
by selecting column G

490
00:54:09,920 --> 00:54:15,280
and going to home on the ribbon, just to
show you a different way to achieve the
same sort of thing.

491
00:54:15,280 --> 00:54:21,520
And I'm going to choose to format the
values as numbers to two decimal places in
this case.

492
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:26,880
So that's just a different way of
formatting something rather than right
clicking in the selection

493
00:54:26,880 --> 00:54:34,560
and going to format cells. Finally, with
this performance index calculated, we can
chart it.

494
00:54:34,560 --> 00:54:41,680
And as before, I'm just going to insert a
2D line chart quickly, reposition it and
size it to

495
00:54:42,320 --> 00:54:48,800
sort of this kind of size, and then select
the appropriate data sources by right
clicking in the

496
00:54:48,800 --> 00:54:57,040
chart area and going to select data
sources. The data series that we need to
add is the CEW index,

497
00:54:57,040 --> 00:55:04,400
with the title of the series set to cell
G1 and the data value selected as cell G2
down to the end of

498
00:55:04,400 --> 00:55:10,560
the series. And after that, of course, we
need to make sure to add our horizontal
axis labels as well,

499
00:55:11,360 --> 00:55:18,240
which are determined by the corresponding
dates to those values for the index that
we've just created.

500
00:55:18,240 --> 00:55:23,520
So the dates will just be, the date
reference will just be from cell A2
downwards, and then we can

501
00:55:23,520 --> 00:55:33,600
press OK twice to view the chart. Okay,
let's have a quick recap here. What we've
just done is basically

502
00:55:33,600 --> 00:55:40,400
calculate or create a constantly equally
weighted index of performance, which has
been based at a value of

503
00:55:40,400 --> 00:55:49,200
100 on the 7th of November, 2014. The
index is made up from the combined
performance of two individual

504
00:55:49,200 --> 00:55:55,840
positions, long fresh pet and short dollar
tree. And to get to this point, we needed
to calculate

505
00:55:55,840 --> 00:56:01,120
the returns for each of those assets over
every period in our time series, and then
combine them

506
00:56:01,120 --> 00:56:09,440
by applying a 50% weighting to the long
FRPT returns and a 50% weighting to the
short DLTR returns. And then

507
00:56:09,440 --> 00:56:16,080
we added those together. Now I'm going to
come back to how this way of measuring
performance differs to

508
00:56:16,080 --> 00:56:20,640
those we've covered previously in a
minute. But first, what I want to address
is something I said

509
00:56:20,640 --> 00:56:27,760
